A listing in the agent's cart sold out a moment ago. Pick substitutes for it out of the remaining records shown, using these reading rules: For a requirement R on property P with value V: a field of the listing settles P whenever its key K names P (K spelled any way; one list member suffices; synonyms are fine and units may be converted; a value that includes V's words is fine, as a shade word followed by the wanted colour satisfies that colour; a limit includes its own boundary); a value filed under a key prefixing P with the item's own kind (on a backpack, a key for the backpack's colour) still counts though the bare key for P disagrees; a key for another property, however related, decides nothing (a strap's colour says nothing about the main body's colour); with no field naming P, V stated under some other field, or in the title or description, is enough, but a lone figure that never says what it measures is worth nothing; when R is a bound expressed with a unit (a measured limit), the only attribute that can settle it is one whose name has some word in common with P, and a figure under an attribute for some other dimension, or solely in the title, leaves R unknown, so the listing fails R; an attribute whose name is P in plural form and whose value is a list enumerates the variants the seller offers, and such a list does not give this particular listing's value whenever the title names some other member of it; if the category label states R, that category meets R; the category label has to match the item; a requirement to differ from another listing's value is taken as met, except where the records reveal that very value; a listing that is out of stock, or that goes over the budget, is not a fit; the binding requirement list overrides the request text, and any requirement flagged DROPPED is void.
Reply with one JSON object only: "fits" (a list of entries, each with "id" (a listing id) and "price" (its price in number form, the way the record states it), covering every listing that seems to satisfy all requirements of the lost line's item, 6 at most) and "reason" (one sentence).

